Okay, had another brainstorm for the next version, that I'll throw out
if anybody's interested. This is taken from a memorable D&D game I
participated in many years ago.

If you pray enough at your altar, your god asks you whether "you wish to
go on the Sacred Quest?" If you answer yes....
You get zapped to a special level that is like the "Big Room" level
described in my spoiler file (but which I've never seen): the whole
screen is just one big room, with maybe some boulders scattered at
random for cover and obstacles. You are at one end, surrounded by 12 to
20 Holy Warriors, who are creatures appropriate to your god's alignment
(trolls and whatnot for chaotic, centaurs and ? for lawful, ? for
neutral?). At the other end is an altar of your alignment, on which is a
Holy Ark (large chest, that is).
Your job is to lead the Holy Warriors in recapturing the Ark from the
forces holding it. They are, naturally, warriors of another alignment.
You are the general of your side's forces; there is a very high-level
general leading the other side (like a demilich or Asmodeus or ?). Of
course, this is a fight to the death.
What's different about this special level is that you direct the
assault. That is, you can tell each of your warriors which enemy
fighters to attack (or you can just lead the charge and let the melee be
at random). This could be done fairly simply with something like the
current "teleport to where?" or / interfaces.
Now, to make this be worth having, I think, there would have to be a
variety of diferent creatures on each side (rather than 20 orcs against
20 trolls, say). So, you as the general have to stop and think about
this for a moment - got to make sure your red naga doesn't waste its
time on a fire-resistant creature, and you'll want to have somebody else
occupy that orange dragon since you don't have a shield of resistance,
etc. Of course, you'll want to be careful about how you use that wand of
lightening or cold, because you don't want to take out your own
warriors.
The enemy creatures should be pretty tough, and should outnumber yours
by a decent margin, and should be predisposed to
go straight after you, so this would be a mission a 5th-level character
would likely not want to undertake. This is something to break up the
monotony of levels 12 to 25 or so for charcters who have had some
success so far. Like 10th-level or so, I'm thinking. And it should
certainly not always include the same creatures, so it's different every
time you play the game.
Should you succeed in slaughtering the enemy, and sacrifice one of them
on the altar, your god of course rewards you for leading the Forces of
Light (or whatever) to victory. I'm thinking maybe one wish, an
armor-class bonus like the priests give for gold, a bunch of gold, an
experience level, and in the Ark, a half-dozen or so random items. Oh,
and you would as general get all the experience for all the enemies
killed in the battle, whether you actually did the deed or one of your
warriors did.
You could abort if things are going badly, and get teleported back to
where you were in the dungeon, but your god will be very angry with you
(lots of negative luck), and you would start aggravating monsters, and
all freindly creatures would henceforth attack you on sight, and one or
two items in your pack would get cursed.

This might be a bit farfetched, but it could provide a unique new
challenge for the game. I think one of NetHack's strengths is miniquests
in the game (like the castle level).

Another, much more modest idea: a wand of rust. If you engrave with it
or zap yourself with it, your armor rusts, but if you zap a human
creature with it you reduce their armor class by one or destroy their
weapon (includes humanoids like orcs, etc.).


